---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const headline = 'How to Create a Landing Page That Converts';
const description =
	'A landing page is one promise and one action. How to match the ad that sent the visitor, structure the page, write a headline that survives a two-second scan, and test without fooling yourself.';

const faq = [
	{
		q: 'What is the difference between a landing page and a homepage?',
		a: 'A homepage serves everyone and routes them; a landing page serves one traffic source and asks for one action. That is why landing pages usually have no site navigation — every link is a way to leave before converting.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How long should a landing page be?',
		a: 'Long enough to answer the objections that stop the action, and no longer. A free download may need 300 words; a high-ticket service may need 1,500. The test is not word count but whether a sceptical reader still has an unanswered question at the end.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How much traffic do I need before A/B testing?',
		a: 'More than most small sites have. Comparing two variants reliably usually needs thousands of conversions, not thousands of visitors. Below that, test one change at a time and judge by direction over weeks, or run qualitative tests — watch recordings, ask five customers what stopped them.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Should a landing page have a form or a booking link?',
		a: 'Whichever has less friction for the buyer. A booking link removes a step for high-intent traffic; a short form captures people who are not ready to book. Two competing calls to action on one page usually reduce both.',
	},
];

const related = [
	{ href: '/guides/carrd-one-page-website/', label: 'How to Build a One-Page Website With Carrd' },
	{ href: '/guides/how-to-build-a-small-business-website/', label: 'How to Build a Small Business Website' },
	{ href: '/guides/google-analytics-4-setup/', label: 'How to Set Up Google Analytics 4' },
	{ href: '/guides/website-launch-checklist/', label: 'Website Launch Checklist' },
	{ href: '/guides/core-web-vitals/', label: 'Core Web Vitals: What LCP, INP and CLS Measure' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
	title={`${headline} | ToolStep`}
	description={description}
	headline={headline}
	canonicalPath="/guides/how-to-create-a-landing-page/"
	published="2026-11-10"
	lead="A landing page is not a short page. It is a page with one job, written for one audience, arriving from one place. Most pages that “do not convert” fail one of those three conditions long before design becomes the issue."
	faq={faq}
	related={related}
	toc={[
		{ href: '#what', label: 'What makes it a landing page' },
		{ href: '#match', label: 'Message match' },
		{ href: '#anatomy', label: 'The anatomy' },
		{ href: '#headline', label: 'Writing the headline' },
		{ href: '#friction', label: 'Form length and friction' },
		{ href: '#testing', label: 'Testing honestly' },
		{ href: '#failures', label: 'Common failures' },
	]}
>
	<div slot="answer" class="answer-box">
		<h2>The short answer</h2>
		<p>
			Repeat the promise from the ad in the headline, show the outcome in the first screen, prove one
			claim with evidence, answer the three objections that stop people, and ask for one action — twice.
			Remove the navigation. Measure the action, not the pageviews.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2 id="what">What makes it a landing page</h2>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Condition</th><th>Implication</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>One traffic source</td><td>You know the ad, search query, or email that sent them, and can mirror its language</td></tr>
				<tr><td>One audience</td><td>No “for businesses and individuals” hedging</td></tr>
				<tr><td>One action</td><td>Every other link is a leak; navigation usually goes</td></tr>
				<tr><td>One measurable outcome</td><td>A form submission, booking, or purchase — not time on page</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		If any of those is missing, you have a web page, not a landing page, and the conversion rate will
		reflect the ambiguity rather than the design.
	</p>

	<h2 id="match">Message match</h2>
	<p>
		The visitor clicked something specific. The first screen has to confirm they arrived where the link
		promised. Match three things:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>The words.</strong> If the ad says “book a call in 15 minutes”, the headline says that — not “Let’s talk”.</li>
		<li><strong>The offer.</strong> If the ad promises a template, the page must be about that template, not the whole product.</li>
		<li><strong>The visual.</strong> The hero image should resemble the creative, so the page feels like the same campaign.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Breaking message match is the most expensive mistake on this list, because it happens before the
		visitor reads anything. They do not evaluate the page; they leave.
	</p>

	<h2 id="anatomy">The anatomy</h2>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Block</th><th>Job</th><th>Typical mistake</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Headline + subhead</td><td>State the outcome and the mechanism</td><td>A slogan that could belong to any company</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Primary CTA</td><td>Visible without scrolling</td><td>“Learn more” instead of the actual action</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Proof</td><td>One credible, specific piece of evidence</td><td>Five anonymous five-star quotes</td></tr>
				<tr><td>What you get</td><td>Concrete deliverables, scope, timing</td><td>Feature list with no outcome attached</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Objections</td><td>Price, commitment, fit, risk</td><td>Hiding pricing behind “contact us”</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Second CTA</td><td>Catch the reader who is now convinced</td><td>No repeat, so they scroll back up</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Microcopy</td><td>Set expectations: what happens next, how long it takes</td><td>Nothing — so the form feels like a commitment</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>

	<h2 id="headline">Writing the headline</h2>
	<p>
		Three questions, answered in order, produce a usable headline:
	</p>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Who is this for?</strong> Make the audience explicit — “for freelance designers”, “for Shopify stores”.</li>
		<li><strong>What changes?</strong> The outcome, not the feature: “invoices that get paid without chasing”.</li>
		<li><strong>What makes it believable?</strong> The mechanism or the timeframe: “one template, ten minutes, no plugin”.</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		Then cut it to one sentence. A useful test: if a stranger reads only the headline, can they say what
		the page offers and to whom? If not, the page is relying on the reader to do work they will not do.
	</p>

	<h2 id="friction">Form length and friction</h2>
	<p>
		Every field costs completions, but the cost is not equal. Ask for the minimum needed to continue the
		conversation, and move the rest to the call or the checkout.
	</p>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Field</th><th>Keep it when</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Email</td><td>Almost always — it is the delivery mechanism</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Name</td><td>You will use it in a reply, or a person will call</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Phone</td><td>The offer is a call or a quote, and you will actually ring</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Company</td><td>You qualify by size or industry before replying</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Budget</td><td>You genuinely cannot serve everyone — offer ranges, not a blank field</td></tr>
				<tr><td>“How did you hear about us?”</td><td>Never on the primary form; ask in the follow-up</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>

	<h2 id="testing">Testing honestly</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Fix the obvious first.</strong> Message match, a visible CTA, and a working form will move more than any A/B test on button colour.</li>
		<li><strong>Test one variable at a time.</strong> Headline, offer, form length, and hero image are the four worth testing, in roughly that order.</li>
		<li><strong>Know your sample size limits.</strong> Small sites should judge direction over weeks, not significance over days.</li>
		<li><strong>Watch recordings and read the exit pages.</strong> Qualitative evidence explains the “why” that a conversion rate cannot.</li>
		<li><strong>Track the whole funnel</strong> — click, form start, form submit. A drop between start and submit is a form problem, not a traffic problem.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="failures">Common failures</h2>
	<div class="warn-box">
		<h2>What actually kills conversion</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Navigation on the page.</strong> Every menu item is an exit.</li>
			<li><strong>Two equal calls to action.</strong> “Book a demo” and “Download the guide” split the intent.</li>
			<li><strong>A form that fails silently.</strong> The visitor sees no confirmation and assumes it broke — which it did.</li>
			<li><strong>Unverifiable claims.</strong> “Trusted by thousands” with no names, numbers, or logos.</li>
			<li><strong>Slow mobile load.</strong> A hero video that takes six seconds reads as a broken page.</li>
			<li><strong>No thank-you page.</strong> Without one, you cannot measure the conversion or tell the visitor what happens next.</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
	<p>
		Build the page in the same platform as the rest of the site if you can — a separate tool adds a second
		place to maintain and a second analytics install. If it is a single page with one action,
		<a href="/guides/carrd-one-page-website/">Carrd</a> is often enough; for anything tied to a larger site,
		keep it on the main domain so tracking and cookies stay consistent. The
		<a href="/guides/google-analytics-4-setup/">GA4 setup guide</a> covers recording the conversion as an
		event rather than inferring it from pageviews.
	</p>
</GuideLayout>
